ion  like turning off the light  [Music]  the morning will be fine for most but  there'll be showers around mainly for  northern and central parts expect some  winter-ness on the hills in the far  north it shouldn't feel quite as cold as  yesterday with the raw wind becoming  confined to north sea coastal areas  the afternoon we'll see showers fading  but they'll keep going over wales and  central england into the evening  the clean cold air means that the air  pollution index where one means low  pollution and 10 means very high will be  low everywhere  the air quality report sponsored by  phillips air purifiers  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news now we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day now with  economist vicki price and policy  director at the grantham research  institute on climate change bob ward so  good to see you both good evening to you  both  so with just 10 days to go until cop 26  it's been confirmed that india's prime  minister narendra modi will travel to  glasgow for the summit downing street  has welcomed the news saying india has a  vital role in making the event a success  but who really holds the power to create  change so bob how significant do you  think it is that narendra modi is  heading to glasgow  well it is very significant because it  raises the prospect that he may make a  major announcement about india's  emissions at the moment we haven't seen  a revised more ambitious pledge from  india which all countries were supposed  to bring forward before glasgow but the  fact that he'll be in glasgow in person  means that he may make an announcement  and some of the discussion has been  about the possibility of india  committing to a net zero target getting  its emissions down to zero perhaps by  2060. so all of this is very exciting  well yes that's right and it's true  isn't it that india hasn't yet announced  plans to reach net zero so vikki  india's under pressure to make that move