 sector and  the public sector and we all benefit if  india does start to limit its emissions  okay we're going to move on to the next  topic now and earlier on the show we  heard how college leaders are calling on  the government to make lessons on global  warming compulsory they say the uk is  unlikely to reach its net zero target  without change so how do we put climate  change into the curriculum um vicky to  you first of all then and just to give  you a statistic less than one percent of  students are on a course with broad  coverage of climate education you can  see that fact there so how important do  you think it is to have climate change  in education for those over 16.  i think it's very important but of  course there are two aspects of climate  change education one is to make people  aware of what the issues are so they  behave in a different way themselves to  the future more responsibly the other  one is to upgrade their skills or make  sure that the skills in the curriculum  that exists  are fit for whatever it is that we want  to do in the future the green jobs that  are being announced for example with a  net zero strategy an extra 450 000  supposedly which has been a figure that  we've been talking about for the past 15  years in my memory um so it is really  whether we're talking about upskilling  and if we know really what those skills  are likely to be needed and which they  are going to be needed in the future  that we not quite sure at present given  that technology is still  unclear and uh there could be all sorts  of areas of activity that will be taking  place in the future that we can't even  imagine right now so the question is  what do we upscale our people for and  are we basically talking about  everything being done in a green way in  other words  having a lot less emission for every  ounce of  production that we have in the future  rather than the way we do things at  present so a bit of an unknown there if  you're going to be teaching it what  exactly are you going to be telling your  students bob do you think that's a fair  point  yes but it's important to remember that  students before 16 already  are taught about climate change as part  of the national curriculum both in  science and in geography so beyond 16  it's a conversation about what how to  put it in not just in science and in  environment but also in in other  subjects such as economics and law and i  know that students at lse for instance  london school of economics where i am  really do want to learn more about  climate change and the way it's  affecting all aspects of our lives and  our economy well that's so interesting  isn't it vicky and some people might say  this this idea is pushing at an open  door i mean we've seen all the school  strikes a lot of those children actually  younger than 16 but there is an  enthusiasm you might say amongst younger  people that it's perhaps not there in  some of the older generations  the interesting thing is that when you  do surveys about climate change and  awareness and whether you want to do  something about it we find that all  generations in the uk certainly seem to  be quite committed to it and understand  the issues so it's not just confined to  any particular age group but what  worries me is that there has been this  huge cut in funding for further  education in other words  risking the older population those who  may need to change their jobs or the way  they do their jobs and that funding  needs to be restored if we are at all  serious about ensuring that population  we have at prison who is in the labor  force uh and always about to get into  the labor force is able to deal with  things for the future okay vicki price  bob ward we're out of time good to see  both thanks very much indeed for joining  us at this evening  that's everything from us for today  joining us on the show tomorrow is  climate activist michaela loach and tom  burke chairman of the e3g think tank  do stay tuned here on sky news though  because coming up next as the daily  number of covered cases tops at 50 000  the prime minister resists calls for new  restrictions and instead urges people to  get a booster jab do stay tuned see you  next time  [Music]
 

